db/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517621} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4c5933-33d1-59b9-ac6d-fe94b7f8b82a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e36e927d-aa55-5840-81c8-aa7b916f3c2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-254913" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[5,\\,3]^{T}$ and $b=[9,\\,-9]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|5-9|,|3-(-9)|) \\\\  & =max(4,12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c9c3102-21e9-590d-ad14-c09baba8b4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Chebyshev Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6e500d-6110-570c-ab83-90ca0a6cd642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c6e500d-6110-570c-ab83-90ca0a6cd642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517571} :answer-field/id #uuid "5175a214-a64f-5932-be81-c533d3570807" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79a48270-e1b9-5009-9c26-f6b63d86b07c" :question/math-academy-id "q-254915" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[0,\\,-6]^{T}$ and $b=[0.5,\\,-7]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|0-0.5|,|-6-(-7)|) \\\\  & =max(0.5,1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
